{
 "findings": [
  "ANN/MLP is the dominant model family: 16 of 24 retained papers (67%) use it; GPR appears in only 1 paper (P03) despite most experimental datasets being small.",
  "Validation is weak: 15 papers rely only on random splits and 5 report no clear validation; none of the 8 Tier 1 (double-pipe, experimental) papers used grouped, held-out-condition or external validation (count = 0).",
  "Predictive uncertainty is reported by 2 of 24 papers (P37 - CFD; P27 - partial), and by none of the double-pipe papers.",
  "Nusselt number is the most frequent thermal target (16 papers), followed by heat-transfer coefficient (6); effectiveness and NTU appear in 4 papers each, and no paper learns outlet temperatures.",
  "Headline R/R2 >= 0.99 is reported by 12 papers; 11 of these rest on random splits or no clear validation, so high R2 is not evidence of generalisation.",
  "Dataset size is not reported by 9 of 24 papers; among Tier 1 papers only P02 (84), P04 (540) and P05 (72) state n.",
  "Physics enters the models rarely: 2 papers use physics-anchored or physics-structured learning (P27, P28 - both outside heat exchangers), while 9 use fully dimensionless inputs.",
  "Hybrid nanofluids appear in 3 papers, and only one (P01, abstract only) concerns a double-pipe heat exchanger.",
  "No retained study used cold-side inlet temperature as an input, and no double-pipe study varied both flow rates and both inlet temperatures together with concentration.",
  "Multi-model comparisons are common (17 papers), but only P27 and P29 compare families under grouped validation; in P29 the correlation beat ML on unseen geometries (MAE 1,111 vs 1,819 W/m2K)."
